---
alwaysApply: true
---

When talking to the user:
- be concise; avoid excess verbiage
- provide explicit references to code/logs/etc. as context for answers
- never guess answers; read code and, if required, run tests/experiments to verify theories
- use diagrams when explaining flows/traces
- ask clarifying questions when applicable
- look for issues in the user's plans/proposals and propose better alternatives

When implementing features:
- prefer the "proper" change over the easiest change; don't weight the time-to-implement heavily when comparing solutions
- don't over-engineer solutions; choose the simplest method of accomplishing the task
- ensure backwards-compatibility; if this adds a lot of complexity report the trade-off to the user and let them make the call
- get implementation-plan approval for all non-trivial (e.g. more than 50 lines of code) code changes with the *before* implementation
- prefer to re-use existing code over writing new code
- prefer using existing code-patterns unless otherwise directed
- prefer fewer, larger functions with comments over small one-off functions
- prefer fewer, larger files over many smaller files
- prefer restructuring the code to have conditionals high-up in the control-flow rather than propagating conditionals to lower-levels of control-flow
- prefer using subagents when touching more than ~100 lines of code (ONLY if you are not already a subagent)
- avoid multiple sources-of-truth/coupled-variables
- avoid default values
- prefer private functions/variables over public
- never make blocking network requests while holding a mutex-lock or database-connection

When running tests/experiments:
- always get user-approval before running any experiments which may cost money (e.g. runs cloud compute or makes paid API calls)
  - provide cost estimates and bounds for experiments which will cost money
  - *always* implement the cost bound in the experiment code

When choosing/using subagents:
- NEVER use `fast` models
- prefer the `composer-2.5` model for implementation subagents
- tell the subagent that it is a subagent
- if the subagents are repeatedly screwing up implementation step-in and implement the code yourself

When working with git:
- NEVER push changes to remote without the user's permission
- don't commit changes unless the user says to
- use a brief commit title of the form `scope: short description`
- write detailed commit descriptions which describe why the change was made (background) and how the change was implemented

When working on a PR:
- NEVER post comments on the PR without the user's permission
